[1] What is here
==============================================================================
The directory tree below holds the documentation of the OOFEM package.  All of
the manuals are Sphinx projects; the reference manual is generated by Doxygen.

OOFEM_DOC_DIR
|
|-- oofemInput       - input manual: the structure and format of the input
|                      file, every record and attribute, with examples
|
|-- usermanual       - user manual: installing and running oofem
|
|-- theorymanual2    - theory manual: formulations and solution procedures
|
|-- developerGuide   - programmer's manual: internal structure and how to
|                      extend the code
|
|-- matlibmanual     - material library manual: the available material models
|                      and their parameters
|
|-- elementlibmanual - element library manual: the supported element types
|
|-- python           - python bindings manual (a symlink to
|                      bindings/python/docs)
|
|-- refman           - reference manual, generated from the sources by Doxygen
|
|-- extractorInput   - documentation of the extractor tool (still plain LaTeX)
|
|-- _ext             - Sphinx extensions shared by the manuals (see [4])
|-- _static          - stylesheet shared by the manuals (see [4])
|-- include          - LaTeX macros used by the remaining LaTeX sources


[2] Pre-requisites
==============================================================================
The manuals need Sphinx, the Python documentation generator
(https://www.sphinx-doc.org):

    pip install -U sphinx

The input manual and the top-level index include Markdown pages through MyST,
so also:

    pip install -U myst-parser

The element library manual draws its figures with TikZ.  They are rendered
properly when sphinxcontrib-tikz is installed; without it the build still
succeeds and shows the TikZ source instead.

    pip install -U sphinxcontrib-tikz

Building the PDF additionally needs a LaTeX installation.  On Ubuntu that is
texlive-binaries, texlive-base, texlive-latex-base, texlive-latex-recommended,
texlive-latex-extra, texlive-fonts-recommended and latexmk; texlive-bibtex-extra
provides biblatex.sty, which the manuals carrying bibliographies need.

The reference manual needs doxygen and graphviz, and is produced in HTML only.

The compiled reference manual is not part of a release because of its size; it
can be read online from the OOFEM home page.

Note that latex2html, tex4ht and htlatex are no longer used by any manual.


[3] Generating documentation
==============================================================================
Every manual is built from its own subdirectory.

To build the HTML version:

    cd doc/<manual>
    make html

The result is in _build/html; open _build/html/index.html.

To build the PDF version:

    cd doc/<manual>
    make latex
    cd _build/latex && make all-pdf

The result is the .pdf file in _build/latex.  ("make latexpdf" from the manual
directory does the same thing in one step.)

"make help" from a manual directory lists all the other formats Sphinx offers.

To build the reference manual, in the refman subdirectory:

    make html


[4] Shared configuration
==============================================================================
Settings that must be the same in every manual live in one place, so that they
cannot drift apart between manuals:

  global_conf.py   Project metadata (author, version, copyright) and the shared
                   HTML and figure-conversion settings.  A manual picks these
                   up with "from global_conf import *".

  _static/oofem.css
                   The stylesheet shared by every manual.  This is where the
                   page width and the figure sizing are defined, for all the
                   themes the manuals use.  Do not set page_width or
                   body_max_width in a manual's html_theme_options: the
                   stylesheet loads after the theme and would override them.

  _ext/oofemroles.py
                   The roles mirroring the record markup of the original LaTeX
                   sources -- :descitem:, :elemparam:, :optelemparam:, :param:,
                   :optparam: -- and the "record" directive used for a record
                   syntax block.

  _ext/oofemmath.py
                   Math macros (\mbf, \del, \der, \pard and the manual-specific
                   ones), defined once and fed to both MathJax for the HTML and
                   the LaTeX preamble for the PDF.  Declaring them for only one
                   of the two is what used to break the PDF build.

  _ext/oofemtikz.py
                   The "tikz" directive used by the element figures.

  _ext/oofemlatex.py
                   Shared LaTeX fixes, currently the author list on the title
                   page, which sphinxmanual.cls would otherwise run off the
                   page.

A manual that needs static directories of its own must keep '../_static' among
them, so that the shared stylesheet is still copied.


[5] Troubleshooting
==============================================================================
A source change seems to have no effect
    "make latex" and "make html" reuse the parsed documents cached in
    _build/doctrees, and a stale cache can regenerate the previous output.  Run
    "make clean", or remove _build, and build again.

The PDF build fails but the log looks empty
    The LaTeX logs in _build/latex are ISO-8859 encoded, so grep treats them as
    binary files and prints nothing.  Use "grep -a" on them.

"Undefined control sequence" in a PDF build
    A math macro is known to MathJax but not to LaTeX.  Add it to
    oofem_math_macros in the manual's conf.py rather than to mathjax3_config;
    see _ext/oofemmath.py.

"Unknown graphics extension: .svg"
    pdflatex cannot include SVG.  Provide a PDF of the figure next to the SVG
    and reference it without an extension, as in

        .. figure:: /figures/myfigure.*

    Sphinx then picks the SVG for HTML and the PDF for LaTeX.

A table has one very wide and one very narrow column
    Sphinx only honours the column widths drawn in a grid table when the table
    carries an explicit :widths: option; without it every column gets an equal
    share.  Add, for example, ":widths: 18 82" to the table directive.


